  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n19Z water vapor showed an upper trough over the Great lakes \nextending back to the south and west across the central plains. \nAnother upper trough was over the northern Rockies with shortwave \nridging over the northern plains. At the surface, high pressure over \nthe northern plains was building south through the central plains \nwith a cold front stretching from the Ozarks into southwest OK. \n\nSensible weather in the short term is expected to be mainly \ninfluence by low clouds more than anything else. The upper trough \nand associated energy within it should pass to the east of the \nforecast area this evening. But satellite has an expansive area of \nstratus where the cold air advection is more pronounced. The RAP and \nRRFS have been consistent in bringing the stratus south and west to \ncover the entire forecast area this evening. Forecast soundings also \nso some low level vertical motion within the saturated layer. \nBecause of this have added a mention of drizzle to the forecast. But \nI would feel more confident in drizzle developing if the isentropic \nprogs show a more organized area of lift to go along with the \nforecast soundings. As it is now, isentropic surfaces mainly show \ndownglide within the cold air advection regime. So I don't \nanticipate anything that would measure, but there could be some \nintermittent drizzle overnight and into Friday morning. The other \nchanges to the blend were also a result of the low clouds forecast \nto build in and hang around for some time was to tweak overnight \nlows a degree or two warmer and to adjust Friday highs lower. Clouds \nshould limit radiational cooling overnight while also limiting \ninsolation during the day. So there should be less of a diurnal \nchange in temps for Friday. Models agree in an inversion setting up \nwith the cooler high pressure at the surface limiting mixing. So \nthink the clouds may hang around until Saturday afternoon when the \nsurface ridge weakens and moves east allowing a southerly return \nflow to develop. \n\n00z ensembles and cluster analysis show pretty good predictability \nin the forecast for this weekend and early next week. So the NBM/WPC \nforecast should be pretty good and fit pretty well with what the \ndeterministic solutions. Overall the pattern becomes a little more \nzonal with no obvious synoptic scale forcing to impact the forecast \narea. So dry weather is expected as temperatures gradually trend \nwarmer. Spread increases in the ensembles by mid week as models want \nto amplify the pattern. The increasing spread comes from different \ndegrees and amplification and different locations of the upper \ntroughs. This is seen in the operational GFS and ECMWF where the GFS \nhas a much stronger cold front moving through Wednesday than the \nECMWF has. The forecast sticks with the blend for now which calls \nfor a more temperate weather and will watch later runs to see how \nthe synoptic pattern may evolve.",
